  'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>"How much is a new roof?" is one of the first questions homeowners ask, and the honest answer is that it depends on your specific house. Two homes on the same street in Temple can have very different quotes because of roof size, steepness, the number of old layers, the condition of the wood underneath, and the material you choose. This article breaks down what actually drives roof replacement cost in Temple, shares some clearly illustrative ballpark ranges, and explains how to turn those ballparks into a real number for your home.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Important:</strong> Any dollar figures in this article are general, illustrative ranges meant to help you understand how pricing works. They are not Temple Roofers' prices and not a quote. Material costs, labor, and the details of your roof change the final number. For a figure specific to your house, use our <a href="/tools/roof-replacement-cost-calculator/">roof replacement cost calculator</a> for a rough estimate, then schedule a free inspection for a written estimate.</div>

<h2>How Roofers Measure and Price a Roof</h2>
<p>Roofers price work by the <strong>square</strong>, which is 100 square feet of roof surface. That is roof area, not the square footage of your home. A 2,000-square-foot single-story house usually has more than 20 squares of roof, because the roof overhangs the walls and slopes upward. Steeper roofs have more surface area for the same footprint, and waste from cutting around hips, valleys, and dormers adds to the material order.</p>
<p>Once the roof is measured, an estimator builds the price from a handful of parts:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tear-off and disposal</strong> of the existing roofing.</li>
<li><strong>Decking repairs</strong> where the plywood or plank boards are damaged.</li>
<li><strong>Underlayment</strong>, ice-and-water or leak barrier in valleys and around penetrations, and drip edge.</li>
<li><strong>The roofing material itself</strong>, plus starter strips, ridge caps, and fasteners.</li>
<li><strong>Flashing and accessories</strong> such as pipe boots, vents, and chimney or wall flashing.</li>
<li><strong>Labor</strong>, which scales with difficulty, not just size.</li>
<li><strong>Permits, dumpsters, and cleanup.</strong></li>
</ul>

<h2>The Biggest Factors in Roof Replacement Cost in Temple</h2>

<h3>1. Roof size (number of squares)</h3>
<p>This is the foundation of every estimate. More squares mean more material and more labor. A modest ranch home might be in the low 20s of squares, while a larger two-story house with a complex layout can run well over 40.</p>

<h3>2. Pitch (steepness)</h3>
<p>A walkable low-pitch roof is quicker and safer to work on. Steeper roofs require extra safety equipment, slower work, and sometimes additional crew, so labor costs climb as the pitch climbs. If you want to understand your own roof's slope, our <a href="/tools/roof-pitch-calculator/">roof pitch calculator</a> explains how pitch is measured.</p>

<h3>3. Number of existing layers</h3>
<p>Some older homes, particularly in established parts of central Temple, have two layers of shingles because a previous re-roof went over the top of the original. Removing extra layers takes more time and produces more debris to haul away. Most reputable roofers recommend tearing off down to the decking rather than adding another layer, because it lets them inspect the wood and keeps weight down.</p>

<h3>4. Decking condition</h3>
<p>Nobody can see the decking until the old roof comes off. Soft, rotted, or delaminated sheets need to be replaced, and that is usually priced per sheet. A good estimate will spell out the per-sheet price up front so there are no surprises. Homes that had long-running leaks, poor attic ventilation, or older plank decking with wide gaps are more likely to need some replacement.</p>

<h3>5. Material choice</h3>
<p>The material you choose has a major effect on cost, lifespan, and how the roof handles Central Texas weather:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Architectural (dimensional) asphalt shingles</strong> are the most common choice on Temple homes. See our <a href="/services/asphalt-shingle-roofing-temple-tx/">asphalt shingle roofing</a> page for options.</li>
<li><strong>Impact-resistant (Class 4) shingles</strong> cost more up front but are designed to better withstand hail, and some insurers offer premium discounts for them. Ask your insurance agent whether that applies to your policy.</li>
<li><strong>Standing seam or exposed-fastener metal</strong> costs more than shingles but can last much longer and reflects more summer heat. Learn more on our <a href="/services/metal-roofing-temple-tx/">metal roofing</a> page.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6. Roof complexity</h3>
<p>Every valley, hip, dormer, skylight, chimney, and wall intersection adds cutting, flashing, and labor. A simple gable roof costs noticeably less per square than a cut-up roof with multiple levels, even if both have the same total area.</p>

<h3>7. Number of stories and access</h3>
<p>Two-story homes take longer to load materials onto and to tear off safely. Tight lots, steep driveways, fences, pools, or landscaping that limit where a dumpster or trailer can go also add time.</p>

<h3>8. Permits, disposal, and extras</h3>
<p>Local permit requirements, landfill and dumpster fees, and add-ons like new gutters, upgraded ventilation, or replacing rotted fascia all show up on the final invoice. If your gutters are worn out, replacing them at the same time as the roof can be efficient; see our <a href="/services/gutters-roof-drainage-temple-tx/">gutters and roof drainage</a> service.</p>

<!--cta-->

<h2>Illustrative Cost Ranges (Not a Quote)</h2>
<p>To give you a sense of scale, here are broad, illustrative ranges per square that are commonly discussed for full replacements, including tear-off of one layer and standard accessories. Actual prices in Bell County shift with material costs, demand after big storms, and the factors above.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>Rough illustrative range per square (installed)</th><th>What to know</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>3-tab asphalt shingles</td><td>Roughly $350 to $550</td><td>Less common now; shorter lifespan and lower wind ratings</td></tr>
<tr><td>Architectural asphalt shingles</td><td>Roughly $400 to $700</td><td>The most common choice for Temple homes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Impact-resistant (Class 4) shingles</td><td>Roughly $500 to $850</td><td>Better hail resistance; ask about insurance discounts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Metal roofing</td><td>Roughly $800 to $1,500+</td><td>Higher upfront cost, long service life, reflects heat</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Using those illustrative numbers, a straightforward 25-square roof in architectural shingles might land somewhere around $10,000 to $17,500, while the same roof in metal could be well above that. Add a second layer to remove, several sheets of decking, a steep pitch, or a second story, and the number moves up. These examples are for orientation only. Our <a href="/tools/roof-replacement-cost-calculator/">cost calculator</a> lets you plug in your own size, pitch, and material for a rough estimate.</p>

<h2>Costs That Catch Temple Homeowners Off Guard</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hidden decking damage.</strong> This is the most common change order. Ask for the per-sheet price in writing before work starts.</li>
<li><strong>Ventilation upgrades.</strong> Many houses here have attics that run extremely hot in summer. Correcting inadequate intake or exhaust ventilation can add cost but helps shingles last and can ease the load on your air conditioner. Our article on <a href="/blog/texas-summer-heat-roof-attic-ventilation/">Texas summer heat and attic ventilation</a> goes deeper.</li>
<li><strong>Code-related items.</strong> Drip edge, proper flashing, and other details may need to be brought up to current standards during a re-roof.</li>
<li><strong>Post-storm demand.</strong> After a large hailstorm, demand for crews and materials can spike across Central Texas, which can affect scheduling and pricing.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Does Insurance Cover the Cost of a New Roof?</h2>
<p>If your roof was damaged by a covered event like hail or wind, your homeowners policy may pay for some or all of the replacement, minus your deductible. Coverage varies widely by policy: some pay replacement cost, others pay actual cash value that accounts for the roof's age, and some policies have separate wind and hail deductibles. Roofs that are simply old and worn out are generally not covered.</p>
<p>Two points worth knowing in Texas: you are responsible for paying your deductible, and it is illegal for a contractor to waive, absorb, or rebate it. Also, roofing contractors cannot act as public adjusters. Temple Roofers can document damage with photos, provide a repair or replacement estimate, and meet your adjuster on site if you want us there, but the claim itself is between you and your insurer. For more detail, see <a href="/blog/roof-insurance-claims-texas-hailstorm/">understanding roof insurance claims after a Texas hailstorm</a>.</p>

<h2>How to Compare Roofing Estimates</h2>
<p>A low bid is not always the better deal. When comparing written estimates, make sure each one covers:</p>
<ol>
<li>Full tear-off to the decking, and how many layers are included.</li>
<li>The exact shingle or metal product line, not just "architectural shingles."</li>
<li>Underlayment type and where leak barrier will be installed.</li>
<li>New drip edge, pipe boots, and flashing, rather than reusing old pieces.</li>
<li>Ventilation plan (ridge vent, box vents, intake).</li>
<li>Per-sheet price for decking replacement.</li>
<li>Permits, dumpster, magnet sweep for nails, and cleanup.</li>
<li>Workmanship and manufacturer warranty terms, in writing.</li>
</ol>
<p>If one estimate is far lower than the rest, look for what is missing. Skipped flashing or reused pipe boots are common shortcuts that show up later as leaks.</p>

<h2>Is Replacement Even Necessary?</h2>
<p>Before budgeting for a whole new roof, confirm that you need one. A roof with isolated problems, such as a few missing shingles or a failed pipe boot, may be a good candidate for <a href="/services/roof-repair-temple-tx/">roof repair</a> instead. Our guide to <a href="/blog/roof-repair-vs-replacement-temple/">roof repair vs. replacement</a> walks through how to make that call, and <a href="/blog/how-long-does-a-roof-last-temple-texas/">how long a roof lasts in Temple</a> can help you judge where yours is in its lifespan.</p>

<h2>Planning and Budgeting for a New Roof</h2>
<p>If your roof is aging but not failing, you have the advantage of time. A few ways Temple homeowners make the project easier on the budget:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Get an inspection before it becomes urgent.</strong> Knowing your roof likely has a few years left lets you set money aside instead of scrambling after a leak.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid the post-storm rush when you can.</strong> If your replacement is not storm-related, scheduling outside the busiest weeks after a major hail event can make it easier to get on a crew's calendar.</li>
<li><strong>Bundle related work.</strong> Gutters, fascia repairs, and ventilation changes are often simpler and less disruptive to handle while the roof is already open.</li>
<li><strong>Think about total cost of ownership.</strong> A material that costs more up front but lasts longer, or that resists hail better, may cost less per year over the time you plan to own the house.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Getting a Real Number for Your Home</h2>
<p>Ballpark ranges are useful for planning, but the only way to know what your roof will cost is to have it measured and inspected. Start with the <a href="/tools/roof-replacement-cost-calculator/">roof replacement cost calculator</a> to set expectations, then book a <a href="/free-roof-inspection/">free roof inspection</a> with Temple Roofers. We will measure the roof, check the condition of the shingles, flashing, and ventilation, photograph what we find, and give you a written estimate for <a href="/services/roof-replacement-temple-tx/">roof replacement</a> or repair, whichever makes sense for your house. There is no obligation, and you will have a clear, itemized picture to compare against any other bid.</p>
